  <section|What they are><label|what-they-are>

  The Lean development is organized around about one hundred stated
  obligations. Each is a proposition <verbatim|YM.Bid.Xnn> in the directory
  <hlink|<verbatim|YM/Bid>|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/tree/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Bid>,
  stated for all gauge data, label systems and tables, and each is proved by
  a theorem <verbatim|answer : YM.Bid.Xnn> in its own file
  <verbatim|YM/Work/Xnn.lean> (sometimes with a folder
  <verbatim|YM/Work/Xnn/> of auxiliary modules). The code calls these
  obligations <em|proof requests> (<verbatim|YM.Bid>); this page calls them
  <em|work items>. There are 105 of them, in nine families A to I, with
  28,654 lines in total (the file together with its auxiliary modules). Three
  further entries in <verbatim|YM/Work/> (<verbatim|MY1/>,
  <verbatim|MY3ActiveGeometry>, <verbatim|MY3SmoothSeedSectors>) are not work
  items.

  The proofs are written as constraints, with docstrings of the form "no
  such-and-such is assumed", so the docstrings are often negative. The
  descriptions below are read from the statement of the proposition together
  with the docstring. The statements are assembled in the files of
  <verbatim|YM/Bid> into named structures (for example
  <verbatim|Bid.station>, <verbatim|Bid.Looms>, <verbatim|Bid.Arcs>) which
  take the propositions as hypotheses and produce the objects used by the
  main theorem.

  <section|Why they are a natural skeleton><label|why-they-are-a-natural-skeleton>

  Each work item states one mathematical fact about the limit theory, in
  terms of objects (tables, phases, rotation actions, Hilbert spaces) that
  are defined elsewhere, and has a self-contained proof. Parts IV to VI of
  the proof (infinite volume; phases and reconstruction; symmetry) are
  covered almost entirely by the families D, B, C and A, so the work items
  give a ready division of an exposition of those parts into statements of
  moderate size, with the dependencies between them stated explicitly. The
  families E to I belong to Part III, the renormalization group; they have
  the same form and are listed here for completeness, but they cover only a
  part of that construction, namely the estimates for one
  renormalization-group step.

  The families build on each other in the following order within the limit
  theory: D (the limit table, with derivatives) comes first; then B (phases,
  positivity in each phase); then C (reconstruction, for each phase); and A
  (Ward identity, rotations, Lorentz group), where the boost items A16 and
  A17 enter C09 and the rotation layer A10\UA15 feeds B06 through the
  rotation and orientation data.

  Readings of the statements are marked <em|certain>, <em|probable> or
  <em|tentative> in the sense of the <hlink|glossary|../glossary.tm>: certain
  where the statement is short and self-explanatory, probable where the
  statement and docstring were read but not the full proof, tentative where
  the vocabulary (the code's nicknames) is not resolved. Lean names in code
  spans are explained in the <hlink|glossary|../glossary.tm> where they occur
  there.

