  Part VI is <em|Symmetry: stress tensor and Ward identities> (family A);
  Part V is <em|Phases and reconstruction> (families B and C); Part IV is
  <em|Infinite volume> (family D); Part III is <em|Ultraviolet construction
  (renormalization group)> (families E to I).

  <section|Family descriptions><label|family-descriptions>

  Families are listed in the order in which they build on each other in the
  limit theory (D, B, C, A), followed by the renormalization-group families E
  to I.

  <subsection|Family D: infinite-volume extraction (Part
  IV)><label|family-d-infinite-volume-extraction-part-iv>

  D01\UD06, 6 items, 1,035 lines. These show that the ordered limit (first
  the cutoff, then the volume, along a chosen subsequence) is a limit of the
  lattice tables in a sense strong enough to inherit smoothness. D01\UD03
  give the coefficient budget, control of the discrepancy between separated
  tuples and smeared test functions, and convergence of one-slot forward
  differences to derivatives of the limit kernel. D04 assembles them into
  <verbatim|Org.Arrives>: the limit table is a spanning limit, so label
  derivatives pass to the limit. D05\UD06 give the one-cap derivative bound
  and the compatibility of the limit differential system with reflection and
  signed symmetries; these are used by <verbatim|Bid.healthy> to show that
  the limit table satisfies the Euclidean hierarchy and the orientation law.
  The family is small: the uniform-in-volume estimates (compactness,
  Arzelà\UAscoli) are elsewhere.

  <subsection|Family B: phase decomposition and Osterwalder\USchrader
  positivity (Part V)><label|family-b-phase-decomposition-and-osterwalderschrader-positivity-part-v>

  B01\UB18, 18 items, 3,603 lines. This family builds the
  Osterwalder\USchrader Hilbert space structure of the mixed limit theory and
  splits it into clustering phases. B01\UB03 give the time-slab word
  operators (<verbatim|Spread>), their norm laws, and the <verbatim|Theme>
  (permutation invariance and linearity through the field quotient). B04\UB05
  pass from lattice grids to continuum points and prove reflection invariance
  of the phases; B06 upgrades this to invariance under all of O(4). B07
  produces the phase decomposition itself: the Gelfand characters of the
  commutative algebra of vacuum-compressed operators (<verbatim|Garland>),
  giving S^mix = \<big-int\> S^\<xi\>. B08\UB14 then show that every phase
  inherits reflection positivity, derivatives and stress laws, the one-cap
  smoothness bound, separability and the operator bound. B15\UB18 supply
  estimates on cutoff families (cutoff trains with bounded moments,
  polynomial limits, convergence of character moments, corrected detectors)
  used to move cluster operators between the cutoff and the limit; they sit
  at the boundary of Parts IV and V. All are assembled by
  <verbatim|Bid.Looms> into <verbatim|Phase.Loom>, hence into
  <verbatim|Materializes> (via <verbatim|Phase.Loom.cleanse>); B15\UB18 first
  pass through <verbatim|Bid.episode> and <verbatim|Bid.fees>. Internal
  chain: B04 \<rightarrow\> B05; B06 \<rightarrow\> B07, B09; B15\UB18 are
  used jointly.

  <subsection|Family C: Osterwalder\USchrader to Wightman reconstruction
  (Part V)><label|family-c-osterwalderschrader-to-wightman-reconstruction-part-v>

  C01\UC29, 29 items, 6,193 lines. This family proves analytic continuation
  of the Schwinger functions to Wightman distributions with Poincaré
  covariance and locality, for any table with the Osterwalder\USchrader data.
  C01\UC03 construct the Osterwalder\USchrader vectors and the strict-time
  continuation <verbatim|Waves>; C04\UC06 give its Cauchy\URiemann
  identities, the absolute-frequency identity and the support in the cone;
  C07\UC09 transfer the Ward boost identities to the real Poincaré action on
  the boundary values (C08: the Poincaré push on joint Schwartz test
  functions; C09 combines A16 and C08); C10\UC16 construct the open-tube
  emission (cone thickening, damping, <verbatim|Aurora>, matching with
  <verbatim|Waves>, Lorentz covariance); C17\UC21 glue the two tubes at
  adjacent spacelike pairs by imaginary boosts (the step of
  Bargmann\UHall\UWightman type), which establishes the input for local
  commutativity; C22\UC24 pass from the Euclidean Gram forms to real-time
  operators; C25\UC29 give the tensor density, nulls, locality (C27), strong
  continuity and word insertion. The statements are collected by the
  structure <verbatim|Bid.Arcs> (A16 and C01\UC29), which is proved outright
  as <verbatim|Integrated.reconstructionArcs>. Internal dependencies: C09 on
  C08 and A16; C14 on C10; C26 on C25; C28 on C08.

  <subsection|Family A: stress tensor, Ward identity and rotations (Part
  VI)><label|family-a-stress-tensor-ward-identity-and-rotations-part-vi>

  A01\UA17, 17 items, 5,022 lines. This family turns the Euclidean limit
  table into one carrying a conserved stress tensor and an action of O(4),
  and then of the Lorentz group, on the labels. It has four layers. (i)
  A01\UA03 and A05\UA06 take the Ward premises (<verbatim|Climb.Pre>: the
  shell maps p and spin rows Y that appear when the angular weight of a
  cutoff is differentiated) and prove their algebra: compatibility with caps,
  the unit, mirrors, shell commutation and transversality; A04\UA06 also
  identify their free-Maxwell scaling limits. (ii) A07\UA09 show that the
  limit rows detect the internal Ward kernel, that the differentiated Ward
  identity gives the so(4) brackets, and a pure Lie-algebra lemma. (iii)
  A10\UA15 integrate the brackets to a group action (<verbatim|Port>, with
  O(4) generated by axis reflections, A13), prove the rotation laws, preserve
  the Casimir polynomial (A11, A12), and average the stress tensor to a
  conserved tensorial J' (A15). (iv) A16\UA17 do the same for boosts. The
  statements are assembled by <verbatim|Bid.station>, which builds the
  <verbatim|Org.Twist.Commission> (A01\UA08, A11, A13\UA17 and A09, A10), and
  by <verbatim|Bid.masts>, which builds <verbatim|MaxwellData> (A09, A10,
  A12). Dependencies: A06 needs A04; A11 and A15 need A10; A14 needs A13; A17
  needs A16.

  <subsection|Family E: energy and pressure estimates for a
  renormalization-group step (Part III)><label|family-e-energy-and-pressure-estimates-for-a-renormalization-group-step-part-iii>

  E01\UE09, 9 items, 3,370 lines. These give one step of the renormalization
  group an energy bound per seed. E01 packs macro charges; E02 compares the
  fine link with the tree predictor; E03\UE04 bound the row, column and lane
  sums of the block kernel; E05 gives coarse-row lower bounds in tubular
  charts; E06\UE08 bound the F/I rows, the departures from affected cones and
  the chord size on unaffected cones; E09 assembles them, with the coarse-row
  lower bound <verbatim|Belly> as a remaining input (<verbatim|Bid.EBare>),
  into <verbatim|Pressure.Teller.Grants>. Order: E03 \<rightarrow\> E04
  \<rightarrow\> E06; E01, E03, E04 \<rightarrow\> E07; E01, E02
  \<rightarrow\> E08; E09 last. The assembling statement is
  <verbatim|Bid.issues>, proved as <verbatim|Integrated.pressure_grants>;
  <verbatim|EBare> is the one input not supplied by the family.

  <subsection|Family F: stationary point and Hessian of the
  renormalization-group step (Part III)><label|family-f-stationary-point-and-hessian-of-the-renormalization-group-step-part-iii>

  F01\UF09, 9 items, 5,259 lines. F01\UF05 (with E02) describe the saddle
  point of the block-spin integral: the tree-slice sensitivity (F01), the
  analytic uncut main term (F02), the canonical Hessian on the tree slice
  (F03), the critical branch and its quadratic value (F04), and independence
  of the choice of gauge-fixing tree (F05). F06\UF09 construct the positive
  quadratic-form data of the stack of towers: repair of the positivity margin
  on the three-form row (F06), square roots of positive spectral symbols by
  Laurent series (F07), the stack at uniform widths (F08), and the
  period-by-period algebra (F09). Assembling statements:
  <verbatim|Bid.cospons> (E02, F01\UF04) gives <verbatim|Scene>, used by
  <verbatim|Integrated.stationary_scene>; <verbatim|Bid.one_for_all> (F05)
  transfers station matching between trees; <verbatim|Bid.pinning> (F06\UF09)
  pins the horizon data.

  <subsection|Family G: label polynomials (Part
  III)><label|family-g-label-polynomials-part-iii>

  G01\UG09, 9 items, 2,865 lines. These show that the renormalized lattice
  observables (labels) are given by invariant polynomials in the local
  curvature slots, with controlled cost. G01\UG02 give gauge and
  signed-permutation covariance and analytic recovery of link holonomy from
  radial connection data (<verbatim|Peg>); G03\UG04 give the linearized
  plaquette sampler and its equivariant splitting; G05 builds the real
  invariant slot polynomials keeping the lower degrees; G06 averages them to
  an equivariant section; G07 bounds the polynomial cost.
  <verbatim|Bid.teaching> assembles G01\UG07 into <verbatim|Bid.Learns>
  (<verbatim|Integrated.source_sections>). G08\UG09 are different: they
  propagate cost bounds along renormalization-group histories between matched
  trajectories, and feed the ultraviolet extension rather than
  <verbatim|Bid>.

  <subsection|Family H: fusion of forced activities (Part
  III)><label|family-h-fusion-of-forced-activities-part-iii>

  H01\UH04, 4 items, 847 lines. Combinatorial identities for the activity
  expansion at the start of a renormalization-group step: the gas identity
  for fused activities (H01), the budget of the merger (H02), the
  forced-family and move receipts (H03), and continuity in the parameter at a
  fixed period (H04). They are consumed together by
  <verbatim|Bid.Oust.begins>.

  <subsection|Family I: marker supports and widths (Part
  III)><label|family-i-marker-supports-and-widths-part-iii>

  I01\UI04, 4 items, 460 lines. Bookkeeping for the markers of the cluster
  expansion: support price by covering number (I01), resale of budgets to
  price tagged weights (I02, using I01), the geometry of the centred link
  tiling (I03) and stop-width bookkeeping (I04). <verbatim|Bid.installs>
  assembles I01\UI04 into <verbatim|Input.Metered>.

  <section|All work items><label|all-work-items>

  Lines are the file together with its auxiliary modules. "Builds on" lists
  the other work items used in the proof or named in the statement. The last
  column is the reading of the description.

  <subsection|Family A (Part VI): stress tensor, Ward identity, rotations and
  boosts><label|family-a-part-vi-stress-tensor-ward-identity-rotations-and-boosts>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|id>>|<cell|<strong|part>>|<cell|<strong|what
  it proves>>|<cell|<strong|lines>>|<cell|<strong|builds
  on>>|<cell|<strong|reading>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A01|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A01.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Ward
  premises (Pre) on the limit table imply the two raw shell maps are
  compatible with the label structure: the translation/colour map p commutes
  with inclusions of caps, kills the unit label and is covariant under signed
  axis permutations (mirrors); the spin rows Y are real,
  inclusion-compatible, kill the unit and are
  mirror-covariant.>|<cell|675>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A02|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A02.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Moving
  the centre of the angular weight inside a fixed cutoff gives commutation
  relations between the derivative on labels and the two shell maps:
  Y(d+1)(v)(deriv u) = deriv(Y v u) + sum_c v_c p(u), and deriv(p u) =
  p(deriv u) (ShellCom).>|<cell|309>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A03|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A03.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|All
  positional jets of the transverse-component vector Ward tests: given the
  colour law, the shell commutation and the line-sum identity, both p and the
  label derivative are transverse (annihilate the longitudinal
  direction).>|<cell|204>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A04|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A04.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|For
  the stress tensor J and free-Maxwell scaling systems X, the Ward test
  written with the derivative of the label (ConLap: conservation versus
  Laplacian of the rescaled stress read-out) holds along every scaling
  system; the limit identity is the one for the free Maxwell
  field.>|<cell|184>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A05|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A05.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Using
  finitely many free-slot tests, along every scaling system the rescaled p
  converges to kappa times the Maxwell label derivative and the rescaled spin
  rows converge to kappa times the Maxwell Lie action on spin; both shell
  maps are recovered from the free-Maxwell
  comparison.>|<cell|617>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A06|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A06.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Transport
  of the finite collinear Ward test matrix: along scaling systems the aligned
  rows converge at fixed free coordinates, and in the limit the transverse
  leaves (derivative and p) vanish on the test
  aisle.>|<cell|258>|<cell|<hlink|A04|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A04.lean>>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A07|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A07.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Internal
  Ward kernel detection: given the action law tying the stress to the Maxwell
  data (ActLaw), the limit rows separate points of the internal
  finite-dimensional space (<verbatim|erases>), so a spin-row defect that
  vanishes on all separating rows is zero.>|<cell|291>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A08|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A08.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Lie
  brackets after Ward propagation: the differentiated Ward identity (a
  directional derivative equals minus a spin substitution sum), together with
  <verbatim|erases>, forces the spin rows Y to satisfy the so(4) bracket
  relations (<verbatim|Brackets Y>).>|<cell|249>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A09|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A09.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Algebra
  lemma: any bracket-satisfying family of spin rows obeys the relations
  <verbatim|rel> and the pairwise commutation of the associated wave
  polynomials (Lie/polynomial lemma used for Maxwell
  data).>|<cell|91>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A10|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A10.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|For
  a rotation action R on the table integrating the spin rows
  (<verbatim|Port>), the Lie map satisfies the brackets and is equivariant:
  R.act A (lie g v) = lie (A g A^-1) (act A
  v).>|<cell|117>|<cell|<hlink|A09|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A09.lean>>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A11|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A11.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|(Assuming
  A10.) Reality and signed subgroup: transporting the Maxwell wave polynomial
  by a real change of basis (signed permutation) keeps it in the preserved
  heat-rank space.>|<cell|207>|<cell|<hlink|A10|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A10.lean>>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A12|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A12.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|Limiting
  Casimir selection polynomial: at every degree cap there is a real
  polynomial p in the Lie generators with <verbatim|Works p> (selects the
  limiting Casimir), for a Port rotation action.>|<cell|338>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A13|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A13.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|The
  orthogonal group O(4) is generated by the coordinate-axis reflections and
  rotations (<verbatim|AxisGens = top>): every hyperplane reflection is
  conjugate to a coordinate mirror by rotations.>|<cell|202>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A14|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A14.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|(Assuming
  A13.) Continuation from <verbatim|Port>: if the configuration-space Ward
  identity holds at all separated points, then the integrated rotation action
  U satisfies the rotation laws for the derivative plan and is oriented, i.e.
  the table is O(4)-covariant at label level.>|<cell|422>|<cell|<hlink|A13|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A13.lean>>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A15|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A15.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|(Assuming
  A10.) Averaging the stress over the rotation group (given the Crystal
  premise lie = c^-1 Y) yields a stress tensor J' that is conserved,
  O(4)-tensorial and satisfies the stress
  laws.>|<cell|653>|<cell|<hlink|A10|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A10.lean>>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A16|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A16.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|The
  proper orthochronous Lorentz group is generated by spatial rotations and
  coordinate boosts (<verbatim|beamGens = top>); boosts suffice to bring a
  future unit timelike vector to rest.>|<cell|103>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|A17|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A17.lean>>|<cell|VI>|<cell|(Assuming
  A16.) Boost covariance of the vacuum: for the Ward data Tab and the
  transported actions at each cap, which are representations, the vacuum
  label space is invariant under boosts (<verbatim|wand>), by ODE uniqueness
  along boosts.>|<cell|102>|<cell|<hlink|A16|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A16.lean>>|<cell|probable>>>>>

  <subsection|Family B (Part V): phase decomposition, positivity in each
  phase, cutoff estimates><label|family-b-part-v-phase-decomposition-positivity-in-each-phase-cutoff-estimates>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|id>>|<cell|<strong|part>>|<cell|<strong|what
  it proves>>|<cell|<strong|lines>>|<cell|<strong|builds
  on>>|<cell|<strong|reading>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B01|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B01.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|For
  the unselected (mixed) theory and a time axis, there is a time-slab
  operator family (a <verbatim|Spread> of word operators on the OS Hilbert
  space) with the all-copy property, from a width supplied by the chessboard
  bench.>|<cell|72>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B02|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B02.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Operator-norm
  laws of that Spread: derivatives in the positions are controlled uniformly
  by bounded operator derivatives (<verbatim|A.Laws
  P>).>|<cell|619>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B03|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B03.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|On
  separated words, the compressed operators M are permutation invariant,
  respect unit labels and are linear in each slot, i.e. depend on words only
  through the all-spectator field quotient
  (<verbatim|Theme>).>|<cell|417>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B04|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B04.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Grid
  passage: for generic grids, the lattice expectation of a point word along
  the ordered limit converges to the continuum point limit
  (<verbatim|Grid.Expects>).>|<cell|187>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B05|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B05.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|(Assuming
  B04.) The mixed theory is reflection invariant in the phase sense
  (<verbatim|Phase.reflects>): the time-slab operators commute with time
  reflection.>|<cell|116>|<cell|<hlink|B04|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B04.lean>>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B06|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B06.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|(With
  phase reflection.) Individual invariance of the compressed operators M
  under every orthogonal transformation, in particular all reflections and
  time-space rotations.>|<cell|244>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B07|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B07.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|(Assuming
  B06.) Existence of the phase decomposition: a family of characters
  (<verbatim|Garland>), i.e. points of the Gelfand spectrum of the
  commutative algebra of compressed vacuum operators, indexing phase tables
  p(i).>|<cell|125>|<cell|<hlink|B06|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B06.lean>>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B08|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B08.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Every
  phase table p(i) is reflection positive, including smeared reflection
  positivity and for all axes.>|<cell|210>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B09|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B09.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Every
  phase table inherits the label derivatives, inclusions and the
  stress-tensor laws of the mixed table.>|<cell|516>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B10|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B10.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Fixed-arity,
  fixed-label bounds on all derivatives (<verbatim|Capped>) follow from the
  one-cap statement (<verbatim|OneCap>) for a table respecting the derivative
  plan.>|<cell|107>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B11|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B11.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Each
  phase table p(i) satisfies the one-cap derivative
  bound.>|<cell|91>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B12|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B12.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|The
  compression algebra of the Spread is norm separable (a countable dense
  sequence in the algebra).>|<cell|93>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B13|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B13.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Operator
  bound in a character chamber: for each phase chamber there is an all-copy
  Spread, because a train estimate on the unselected vacuum space controls
  the character chamber.>|<cell|106>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B14|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B14.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|There
  is one countable list of future words whose span is dense in every phase
  chamber (<verbatim|Cluster.horizon>), so countably many tests select the
  ground state.>|<cell|149>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B15|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B15.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Translated
  cutoff trains obey \<\|\|\>E.fromCut\<\|\|\> \<less\>= (g+eps)^(2n) at late
  cutoffs, under a centred episode budget g
  (<verbatim|Sold>).>|<cell|131>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B16|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B16.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|For
  fixed train and degree, the finite polynomial expectation along cutoffs
  converges to the limit expression <verbatim|wanted> (finite polynomial
  limits).>|<cell|195>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B17|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B17.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Character
  moment limit: the limiting train expectation equals \<less\>Omega,
  ((cluster)^* cluster)^n Omega\<gtr\> in the OS Hilbert space (moment
  convergence to the vacuum moments of the cluster
  operator).>|<cell|91>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|B18|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/B18.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Episodes
  for corrected detectors: polynomial detectors encoded as episodes send the
  cluster-end quantity to the cutoff-end quantity (<verbatim|Sends>), for
  both the one-step and two-step differences.>|<cell|134>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>>>>

  <subsection|Family C (Part V): Osterwalder\USchrader to Wightman
  reconstruction><label|family-c-part-v-osterwalderschrader-to-wightman-reconstruction>

  <block|<tformat|<twith|table-width|1par>|<twith|table-hmode|exact>|<cwith|1|-1|1|-1|cell-hyphen|t>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-bborder|1ln>|<cwith|1|1|1|-1|cell-valign|bottom>|<table|<row|<cell|<strong|id>>|<cell|<strong|part>>|<cell|<strong|what
  it proves>>|<cell|<strong|lines>>|<cell|<strong|builds
  on>>|<cell|<strong|reading>>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C01|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C01.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Centred
  insertions on the common label union give a <verbatim|Small> family of
  vectors in the OS Hilbert space and the <verbatim|Piece> property
  (all-spectator matrix elements).>|<cell|119>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C02|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C02.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|(Assuming
  C01.) The Small family has the slicing property (<verbatim|Slice>): one
  strict-time width can be used for the whole multilinear
  form.>|<cell|179>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C03|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C03.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Strict-time
  analytic continuation: there is a family of continuation functions
  <verbatim|Waves> (holomorphic in complex times) directly computed from the
  OS Gram forms.>|<cell|260>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C04|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C04.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|The
  continuation has Cauchy-Riemann identities at strictly ordered imaginary
  time heights, with Cauchy bounds on slices and integration by parts in the
  time line.>|<cell|224>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C05|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C05.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Absolute-frequency
  identity on compact Schwartz tests: pairing the continuation with the
  exponentially weighted frequency tests gives the boundary distribution
  (<verbatim|during>).>|<cell|241>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C06|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C06.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Half-space
  (spectral cone) support: the boundary distribution of the continuation has
  frequency support in the cone (<verbatim|cone_tests>).>|<cell|159>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C07|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C07.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Temporal
  PDE on joint Schwartz functions: Ward boosts on strict-height imaginary
  times give the infinitesimal boost identity for the continuation
  (<verbatim|Boosty>).>|<cell|673>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C08|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C08.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|The
  Poincare group acts continuously on joint Schwartz tests (strong topology)
  and the boost generator is the derivative of the
  push-forward.>|<cell|288>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C09|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C09.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|(Assuming
  A16, C08.) From Ward boosts on imaginary times to real Poincare motion: the
  boundary Wightman distributions are translation and Lorentz invariant with
  the label rotation action (<verbatim|Urs>).>|<cell|443>|<cell|<hlink|A16|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/A16.lean>,
  <hlink|C08|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C08.lean>>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C10|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C10.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|The
  spectral cone is separated by relative gaps (<verbatim|Wide>):
  absolute-coordinate tube dual positivity.>|<cell|72>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C11|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C11.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Existence
  of a smoothed thickened-cone cutoff (<verbatim|Blanket>) with a compactly
  supported unit-integral bump.>|<cell|102>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C12|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C12.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Damping
  on the closed shift set: the exponential lens is controlled in every
  Schwartz seminorm near the cone (<verbatim|Modified>).>|<cell|183>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C13|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C13.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Analytic
  emission in the open tube: there is an emitted function (<verbatim|Aurora>)
  holomorphic on the whole open tube, given the
  cutoffs.>|<cell|456>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C14|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C14.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Identification
  of the strictly damped contour as a function (<verbatim|Income>): the
  emission is a genuine function at all real
  coordinates.>|<cell|262>|<cell|<hlink|C10|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C10.lean>>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C15|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C15.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|The
  emission matches the continuation <verbatim|Waves> at ordered complex times
  (<verbatim|FullBody>).>|<cell|133>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C16|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C16.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Real
  Lorentz covariance inside the tube for the emission
  (<verbatim|wave_turn>).>|<cell|93>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C17|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C17.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Small
  imaginary boosts at an adjacent spacelike pair: heights tending to zero
  along which a boost path (<verbatim|travelable>) connects the two
  tubes.>|<cell|286>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C18|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C18.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|(Assuming
  <verbatim|Descends>.) The boost action on labels extends analytically to
  complex rapidity (<verbatim|Weighty>).>|<cell|80>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C19|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C19.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Germs
  through each tube from real Lorentz covariance and the boost path: both
  tube germs (<verbatim|Panes>) exist at an adjacent spacelike
  pair.>|<cell|228>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C20|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C20.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|The
  two germs agree at a Euclidean point (<verbatim|sameGround>), by jet
  agreement on a totally real slice and analyticity.>|<cell|309>|<cell|\U>|<cell|certain>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C21|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C21.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Local
  realization: both partial boundary values are realized as partial
  distributions on a neighbourhood (<verbatim|Panes.Forms>).>|<cell|88>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C22|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C22.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Inner
  products at separate heated profiles: Gram forms of the OS vectors continue
  to the Wightman pairing (<verbatim|Hull>).>|<cell|355>|<cell|\U>|<cell|probable>>|<row|<cell|<hlink|C23|https://github.com/yang-mills-group/OpenAI-YM-20261028/blob/3456e5e75cf8d4cd558955a5c7638fba2d68f464/YM/Work/C23.lean>>|<cell|V>|<cell|Fixed-height
